From: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
Subject: [PATCH] input: rsinput: frame the RX stream instead of the batch

rsinput_rx() treated each serdev receive callback as exactly one complete
frame: it copied the batch into rx_buf, took the last byte as the checksum,
validated it across the whole batch, and dropped everything on a mismatch.

serdev does not frame anything. It hands over whatever the tty layer has
accumulated, so a batch can hold two frames back to back, a frame preceded by
line noise, or a partial frame. In each of those the batch-wide checksum fails
and every frame in it is thrown away, valid ones included.

That is why the MCU version handshake fails on resume. The MCU is power-cycled,
the reset and enable transitions put noise on the line, and the version reply
arrives in the same batch. dmesg shows "Checksum mismatch" immediately before
each of the three "MCU version handshake timed out" warnings, and the resume
ends in

  rsinput serial1-0: PM: dpm_run_callback(): rsinput_resume returns -110
  rsinput serial1-0: PM: failed to resume: error -110

after burning the full 1.5s retry budget, which is over half of the in-kernel
resume time on this device.

rsinput_process_data() already walks a batch frame by frame using the declared
length and validates each checksum on its own, skipping only the frame that is
actually bad. Drop the batch-wide check in rsinput_rx() and let it.

Add header resynchronisation to that walk while here. It read a length field
without first confirming it was looking at a frame boundary, so a batch that
starts mid-frame or with noise misparses. The header is four fixed bytes, so
walk forward until it matches.

This also fixes silent input loss during normal play: any time two gamepad
frames landed in one callback, both were discarded.

Signed-off-by: PortareOS <hello@portare.org>
---
diff --git a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
index 9c99f74..fcbe187 100644
--- a/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
+++ b/drivers/input/joystick/rsinput.c
@@ -445,8 +445,24 @@ static void handle_cmd_status(struct rsinput_driver *drv, const uint8_t *data, s
 
 static void rsinput_process_data(struct rsinput_driver *drv, const uint8_t *data, size_t len) {
     while (len >= MCU_PKT_SIZE_MIN) {
-        uint16_t payload_length = data[FRAME_POS_LEN_L] | (data[FRAME_POS_LEN_H] << 8);
-        size_t frame_length = MCU_PKT_SIZE_MIN + payload_length;
+        uint16_t payload_length;
+        size_t frame_length;
+
+        /*
+         * Resynchronise on the frame header before reading anything else. A
+         * receive batch can start mid-frame, or with line noise from the MCU
+         * power cycle at resume, and a length read out of that lands anywhere.
+         * The header is four fixed bytes, so walk forward until it is found.
+         */
+        if (data[0] != FRAME_HEAD_1 || data[1] != FRAME_HEAD_2 ||
+            data[2] != FRAME_HEAD_3 || data[3] != FRAME_HEAD_4) {
+            data++;
+            len--;
+            continue;
+        }
+
+        payload_length = data[FRAME_POS_LEN_L] | (data[FRAME_POS_LEN_H] << 8);
+        frame_length = MCU_PKT_SIZE_MIN + payload_length;
 
         if (len < frame_length) {
             return;
@@ -483,7 +499,6 @@ static void rsinput_process_data(struct rsinput_driver *drv, const uint8_t *data
 
 static size_t rsinput_rx(struct serdev_device *serdev, const u8 *data, size_t count) {
     struct rsinput_driver *drv = serdev_device_get_drvdata(serdev);
-    uint8_t received_checksum, computed_checksum;
 
     if (!drv || !data || count == 0) {
         dev_warn_ratelimited(&serdev->dev, "Invalid RX data\n");
@@ -497,20 +512,19 @@ static size_t rsinput_rx(struct serdev_device *serdev, const u8 *data, size_t co
 
     memcpy(drv->rx_buf, data, count);
 
-    if (count < MCU_PKT_SIZE_MIN) {
-        dev_warn_ratelimited(&serdev->dev, "Frame too short for checksum validation\n");
-        goto error;
-    }
-
-    received_checksum = drv->rx_buf[count - 1];
-
-    computed_checksum = compute_checksum(drv->rx_buf, count);
-
-    if (computed_checksum != received_checksum) {
-        dev_warn_ratelimited(&serdev->dev, "Checksum mismatch\n");
-        goto error;
-    }
-
+    /*
+     * No checksum here. serdev hands over whatever the tty layer has
+     * accumulated, which is not necessarily one frame: it can be two frames
+     * back to back, a frame behind power-cycle noise, or a partial frame.
+     * Checksumming the batch as though it were a single frame fails in all
+     * three cases and discarded the whole thing, valid frames included. That
+     * is what made the MCU version handshake at resume time out three times
+     * and give up with -ETIMEDOUT.
+     *
+     * rsinput_process_data() already walks the batch frame by frame and
+     * validates each checksum on its own, dropping only the frame that is
+     * really bad. Let it.
+     */
     rsinput_process_data(drv, drv->rx_buf, count);
 
 error:
